{"content": "/**
 * test-builds-first.test.ts — a script that runs tests which read `dist/` builds it
 * first.
 *
 * ## The failure this exists for
 *
 * `dist/` is this repository's build output and is gitignored, so a checkout does not
 * have it. Eight of the contract suites and four e2e suites read it — the generated
 * workflows, the release ruleset, the bundled tool servers — because the artifact is
 * what they are about.
 *
 * CI was never affected, which is the whole problem: `checks.from_pull_request` in
 * `.github/atomaton/config.yaml` runs `synth` before `test`, so the build happened in
 * the one place somebody was watching. An agent's run does not build: the runner runs
 * `environment.setup_commands` and nothing else, and that list is install-only.
 *
 * So `bun run test` was `0 fail` in CI and `59 fail` inside every agent's run, every
 * failure an `ENOENT` under `dist/`. Measured on a clean checkout:
 *
 *     rm -rf dist && bun run test
 *      1399 pass / 62 fail   (with ATOMATON_MACHINERY_ROOT set)
 *      1409 pass / 59 fail   (with it unset — the missing directory, not the variable)
 *
 * `bun run synth` takes 0.4s. An agent that does not already know to type it cannot
 * tell those 59 failures from ones its own change caused, which is the same damage the
 * hermetic-environment test beside this one exists for.
 *
 * ## Why the fix is here and not in `environment.setup_commands`
 *
 * Both were open, and this one is the one that works on the branch that changes it.
 * `setup_commands` is read from the DEFAULT BRANCH, so a command added there cannot be
 * exercised by the pull request that adds it — the run reviewing that pull request
 * still has the old list. A `pre<name>` script is in the tree under test, so the person
 * or agent reviewing it can reproduce the fix.
 *
 * It also covers every caller rather than the three jobs this repository happens to
 * have: `test` and `test:e2e` build before they read, whether the caller is an agent's
 * run, a laptop, or a check that runs the script directly. CI pays a second 0.4s build
 * for that, which is the cost of the two environments agreeing.
 *
 * ## What this holds
 *
 * 1. The behavioural half: `bun run test` and `bun run test:e2e` produce a `dist/` that
 *    was not there before, from a tree where it is missing.
 * 2. The ratchet: every `test*` script whose own files name a `dist/` path in code must
 *    build `dist/` first. A `.test.ts` added tomorrow that reads the artifact fails this
 *    rather than failing 20 minutes into somebody's run.
 *
 * ## What this cannot see
 *
 * A `dist/` path assembled from pieces (`join("dist", ...)`) reads the artifact without
 * naming it, and a file that reads `dist/` through a helper in another file is found
 * only by the name in the helper. Both directions are text scans, in the same shape as
 * `machinery-root.test.ts` beside it, and the limits are the same.
 */
import { describe, expect, test } from "bun:test";
import { spawnSync } from "node:child_process";
import { existsSync, readFileSync, readdirSync, rmSync, statSync } from "node:fs";
import { join } from "node:path";

/** Every script in package.json, by name. */
function scripts(): Record<string, string> {
